A||||8||dogtired||What Year Is this Beretta??||||6||
Z||000000||dogtired||03-22-2000||11:07 AM||||By the topic name you wouldn't think this is Fiero related but is!<BR>Here's the Story <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>While laid up with my back surgery, I got a call from a fellow list.Forum member. Question was did I know anyone who might be interested in his 86SE. Well I ended up buying it  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> and with it came a Bretta VIN# 1G1LV14W9JL577738. I didn't get a title with the Bretta but it does have a good 2.8 in it. Searched Beretta on the Net but didn't find any Vin decoder for them. Just thought someone might have an idea or know of a place I could find out???<BR>Even though this is a front wheel drive car isn't it a direct bolt in to a Fiero?? Is the starter on the right side. <BR>Due to my cruuent health I have only seen the car once and didn't have enough pain pills to crawl under it to look  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>Just thought someone here might know??<BR>Later Listers!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> THANKS,  the old dog is back  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>||||reg||6
Z||000001||Raydar||03-22-2000||11:39 AM||||Dawg! Glad to see yer back! (Small pun intended.)    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>The Beretta's starter should be on the same side as the Fiero's. The 2.8 may even be a bolt-in. It may not have the big-valve heads that the Fiero has. (Not sure about the Beretta, but the S-10 and possibly the Camaro 2.8 had smaller valves than the Fiero.) The engine electronics will probably be somewhat different.   <P>[This message has been edited by Raydar (edited 03-22-2000).]<p>[This message has been edited by Raydar (edited 03-22-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||DRH||03-22-2000||12:02 PM||||Is this to replace a Fiero L4 or V6? <P>Replacing a Fiero V6 might not be too bad if you just use the basic engine from the Beretta. I believe the Beretta is distributorless so hopefully they still put the gear on the camshaft??? Then you could swap intake, exhaust, distributor, brackets, etc from Fiero.<P>Replacing an L4 will be a lot more work and may require access to a V6 parts car.<P><BR>Oh... I wouldn't swear to it but I believe the 10th digit is the year on all GMs in that era. J would make it an 88.<P><p>[This message has been edited by DRH (edited 03-22-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||Oreif||03-22-2000||02:08 PM||||It has to be 1989 or before. In 1990 the engines were upgraded to a 3.1L.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||TalShino||03-22-2000||02:34 PM||||Its an 88..  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>TalShino||||reg||1
Z||000005||WKDFIRO||03-22-2000||11:30 PM||||Im not 100% sure but I think the Fiero was the only 2.8 with the bigger valves. Everything else was pretty much the same, block wise.||||reg||1
Z||000006||dogtired||03-23-2000||11:33 AM||||My guess is that it's an 88. My thought was just to use the block in a current V6car and bolt the fireo heads and plenum on it??  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>Sounds like a keeper to me, Think I will just drop the motor and junk the rest!<BR>Thanks for all the comments.||||reg||14
Z||000007||mwbackus||03-23-2000||12:24 PM||||You should probably keep the transmission too if its any good.  The 5sp Getrag from the Beretta's will work in a Fiero with a few braket modifications (available from Rodney D.).<P>Mike||||reg||1
Z||000008||Smokey||03-23-2000||03:37 PM||||hey dog....according to kbb.com (kelly blue book) that beretta is an 88, if you'd like to see for yourself just go to the "Lemon Check" on the page and type in your vin# and zipcode||||reg||3
